{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1357869-story-seed-the-boy-without-a-birthday","id":1357869,"title":"Story Seed: The Boy Without a Birthday","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2019-12-30T13:07:57-08:00","id":1357869,"title":"Story Seed: The Boy Without a Birthday","content":"Well, while it's gonna take me bit to put this order for you together-you said you like odd stories and urban legends, right? Well, here's one for you:<br /> <br /> There's always that one kid at a birthday party, you know? Unless it's super private, there's always someone who wanders into the restaurant or in from the neighborhood or what not. Maybe they're curious, or they know exactly what is going on. Some want to steal some food, maybe a gift, some want to feel like they belong, or they legitimately see an opportunity to make friends, or hell, maybe they just want to actually celebrate someone's birthday. Most of them are just people, you know, the facts of life.<br /> <br /> But every once in a while, you get one who's more of a fact than might be obvious.<br /> <br /> Every now and then, there'll be a boy, and he likes to dance. He likes to play soldier and looking at flowers. Scruffy without seeming neglected, seems like he'd be a dreamer if he was doing better. Pretty ordinary kid, sweet, little awkward but kind of instantly loveable, that sort. The parents at the party just have to make the usual assumptions, that he's a kid from school, or the neighborhood. One of the other kid's friend, inevitably.<br /> <br /> Kids don't take as easily to that-not that they aren't sure they know him from somewhere, but they don't have extraneous people in their lives quite the way adults do or think everyone does. He's pretty bad with names, though great with locations, and can always describe a classroom or point out a house it'd make sense for him to be from. Again, it's not like anyone knows exactly where he's from-but he has to come from somewhere, yeah? Everyone's from someplace. A bad assumption, in my experience, but generally true.<br /> <br /> But this kid, he likes birthday parties best of all, even if they always make him a little sad.<br /> <br /> Ah, now you gotta wonder-what kind of kid gets sad at a birthday party? Something bad happen, kid, something jiggle loose from your life?<br /> <br /> No, he'll say meekly. It's just I ain't got one. One what? A birthday.<br /> <br /> Well, that's just nonsense. Everyone's got a birthday.<br /> <br /> I ain't.<br /> <br /> Well, o'course you do. Your parents must have told you at some point, and we can just ask them.<br /> <br /> 'Bout here in the conversation, kid might get a little sketchy, little ready to skate. He'll never back away from the birthday thing, but he'll own to having parents, so long as you don't try and talk to them. Try that, and he'll give you a number. You'll call them and find out your just called Meineke or McDonalds or any of these business what start with an M. Then you'll find out the boy's gone, even if you had a firm grip on his shoulder or wrist. Probably never see them again, the sort of thing that happens all the time. Maybe you'll call the cops on him, but you know how they find missing kids.<br /> <br /> Could be that some of your kids made friends with him, could be you did yourself. So long as you don't press it, he'll show up again, hang out with the kids every now and then, play video games (though he's more of an outdoor kid) or share in the pizza. Never hyper constant, but frequent enough you don't forget him, always enough to keep being glad you know him, you know, in that way you're just glad to have a friendly cashier who sees you regular enough. Always birthday parties, particularly if no one bullies him or chases him away. He gets too much grief, seems like the kid gets the idea.<br /> <br /> But it keeps coming around to it-I ain't got a birthhday. And of course, someone down the line gets a bright idea.<br /> <br /> Course you got a birthday. Everyone's got a birthday. Why don't we have one for you? We'll give you one.<br /> <br /> Someone'll set a date, call up his friends, arrange for things. His parents are busy trying to make ends meet, so he doubts they'll make it, but he'll be sure to ask them if it's okay. You try and call them, you'll get Mother Costa's or Mother Glory Espresso and that's all she wrote. Gonna be a good thing, a sweet thing.<br /> <br /> Day comes, and the party lasts as long as it's supposed to, down to the exact minute. It's a little odd-if it's at a house, kids really don't come to run around the house or nothing. No one really goes in or out at all, you know. You can hear them doing stuff though. If it's a public venue, well, you thought they weren't going to shut it down for a private party, but guess they must have changed their minds-blinds down and all.<br /> <br /> And life almost goes on. Almost.<br /> <br /> Most people won't notice anything different, of the second question variety. You know, another day, another dollar.<br /> <br /> Some people do. Didn't you use to have a dog instead of a cat? No, Frank's allegric. How does a Straight-A, upright Chrisitian girl end up pregnant? We're not quite sure, but we're very embarrassed about it, please just drop it. Didn't you have four kids? No, Kat lost her fourth one in the hospital, how could you forget that? Weren't you a doctor? No, I was studying for my PhD when Erik took me on the coke trip that ended my career. You didn't use to be twins, right? What are you talking about? Almost always, someone's missing.<br /> <br /> Time and tide from there would only reveal weirder. Excuse us ma'am, we're the Belgium Immigration Office, we have a little girl here who can't remember the names or faces of her family but we found on record to be your child. We would like to ask questions in association with the American Child Protective Services. A man shows up one day and breaks down on the porch, weeping that he finally found his mama, that it was so long in the war. Sir, we have thirty orders of gore pornorgraphy here for you from five different locations ordered at the exact same time-we have some questions.<br /> <br /> The young man never quite shows up again. You think you seem him for a while, just as he's disappearing, but he never really manages to stay around long enough for you to catch him, and eventually he's just gone forever.<br /> <br /> Life almost goes on. Most people just accept that these things happen. It does, you know.<br /> <br /> But now, you probably have that &quot;of the second question variety&quot; bit niggling at you. And to be fair, it's hard to ignore. Life finds a way, but it's hard to ignore the first question.<br /> <br /> Namely because you'll very rarely ask &quot;What the hell happened to you?&quot; feeling so faint.<br /> <br /> Maybe they're wounded after the party, maybe they ain't. Could be a corpse, could not be. But when you meet the partygoers, it's clear something done them wrong. Something hurt them, something <i>took</i> something from them. Traumatized, clearly. Something about that party went wrong.<br /> <br /> No one can clearly remember. It seemed normal enough starting off, kids all excited and playing and watching stuff. You don't really remember getting calls or texts-maybe a few early on, but nothing so serious you feel like you need to leave. You have things to do.<br /> <br /> Kids remember more being off at this point. The sink's leaky, didn't Uncle Steve just fix that? Hey, is the corner of this room warped a bit? almost like it's trying to spiral into itself. Was that someone down the hallway? Nah, just a mirror, happens. The kids might want to leave, but the adults know it's not time for that yet-no, gotta blow out the candles and cut the cake first, presents, then outside. But something weird's going on. It's like the party space is coiling up to do something.<br /> <br /> The boy can't be happier, excited by anything and everything-but still a little sad. He doesn't know why he's sad. Maybe it's depression-kids can pick that up early, you know. But man, nothing makes him so excited for the song the go, the cake to come down, and for him to blow out the candles.<br /> <br /> As the kids say, that's when shit hits the fan.<br /> <br /> Again, no one's completely clear on what happens once the candles are blown out. Conventional psychological and psychiatric practices just yield traumatic responses without picking out exact causes. Stimuli tends to lean towards time-keeping, pregnancy, funerals, other strong emotions. Hypnosis, Dream therapy, automatic writing or drawing, in as much as any professional uses such things, yields rooms of wombs, fractal spaces, repeating time-loops, wishes of living and dying-not just mere life or death, but the things that vivify and affect people, that MAKE for a life-come true-like, not when you wish for a remote control car you'll throw away before the year is out, but when your mom just wishes you'd find a girlfriend already, she wants grandkids-and not just when you wish Trump would die because god what a prick, but when you hope Maggie gets AIDS, you didn't raise her to be like that. People remember leaving the house only to wish they didn't, because it just keep looping around getting messier and more....toothsome. Something....almost bacterial, almost insectoid-prowls the space.Almost always, someone finds a version of themselves they don't like. Almost always, someone says something they'll never get to take back.<br /> <br /> And always, a birthday is undone. Could be a death, could be a disappearance. Hard to know. It doesn't play easy rules<br /> <br /> Intervening the time is a call back to the boy blowing out candles-one long breath, but in a sweep that took the candles out one by one. That scene pops up again and again, a unifying interval that brings everyone back together for witnessing, paradoxes spiraling out into the world for each wish, each candle the party thinks his age is.<br /> <br /> Now you're wondering-what about killing the boy? Well, outside of that, if asked, if pushed, the boy apparently says he can be killed. Has been, a lot. He remembers wasting away to smallpox, he remembers starving behind a dumpsters, tripping and spearing his skull on a barb of rebar-most distressingly, remembers being shot , stabbed, choked, poisoned by people trying to fix what his party did. He doesn't like to talk about it. he doesn't know much about what's going on, doesn't even seem to remember unless it's heavily broached, and even then only in a vagueness related to the fact he doesn't have a birthday. He can have as many death days as he wants, but since he ain't got no birthday, nothing seems to stick. An unreal life, without a tie between those two defining moments.<br /> <br /> But in that moment? As the candles go out? Nah, damage is done. It's an echo, an eddy following in the wake of thing that shouldn't have been done. A way to know that reality scanning for errors, that existence is pouring alcohol on the wound just opened, that it'll all be over soon.<br /> <br /> See, my personal theory is that it's <i>rule</i> of the world that that boy can't have a birthday. Something about the celebration process tries to anchor a time to him, but he ain't supposed to have one. The world has rules, but any system or set of rules is gonna have cracks people fall down-but in his case, it just might be he is the crack itself. Whatever the case, he's a boy without a birthday. Ain't supposed to get one, because then that would violate his nature as a boy without a birthday.<br /> <br /> Of course, now, maybe someone can figure out a way to give him one, or to work out all the nonsense that makes him up, that traps and warps those who try to do him a kindness. That's the trouble with hope, idn't it? Maybe someone can fix it all. Maybe that's why people keep having the damn birthday parties.<br /> <br /> Anyways, your total comes out to $562.82. Yeah, I know, college gouges you kids, but hey, I don't make the rules. You have a good day now.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":99,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","user":"ArcaneArts","date":". 12-30-2019, 04:36 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-30T13:28:19-08:00","id":1357875,"title":"","content":"Yeah, who needs sleep anyway... here, keep the change. <br /> <br /> (seriously, a great material for Innocents Chronicle. While it felt very God-Machiney at first, it ended up very abyssal- but I assume there could be many explanations for how does someone doesn't have a birthday. Maybe Ammut ate it, who knows).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-30T13:32:29-08:00","id":1357877,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LostLight</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1357869-story-seed-the-boy-without-a-birthday?p=1357875#post1357875\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah, who needs sleep anyway... here, keep the change.<br /> <br /> (seriously, a great material for Innocents Chronicle. While it felt very God-Machiney at first, it ended up very abyssal- but I assume there could be many explanations for how does someone doesn't have a birthday. Maybe Ammut ate it, who knows).</div> </div> </div> </div>I wrote it to be setting agnostic, though while I was in the flow, I definitely felt a G-M vibe, though I also had some Changeling and Mage vibes through, and even had one moment where I wondered if he was an Arisen who's somehow particularly fucked.<br /> <br /> I think the best way to play the story, though, would be the mortal investigator angle, where it's not anything in <i>particular</i>-just a boy who really wants something, a host of terrible things that happen when you try to give it to him, and the possibility there's something in the details that reveals how to resolve it.<br /> <br /> EDIT: BTW, if anyone wants the song I was listening to, It's....an odd mis-match to it. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=C6bo14aOe1I\" target=\"_blank\">Hero by Lissie.</a>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":99,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","user":"ArcaneArts","date":". 12-30-2019, 04:42 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}